E=[
(1,1862,"Falls","Black rain at Carluke","A black rain falls at Carluke, 140 miles from the black rains of Slains. Fort cites the Rev. James Rust's Scottish Showers, which lists recurring black rains in both places.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Rev. James Rust, Scottish Showers","another at Carluke, 140 miles from Slains, May 1, 1862"),
(2,1869,"Sky","Shining objects before Venus","A correspondent at Northampton, looking at Venus, sees a host of shining objects of varying size. He thought it unlikely they were seeds so early in spring. He watched them about an hour.","NL","New Lands, citing Astronomical Register, 7-138","upon May 2, 1869, he was looking at Venus, and saw a host of shining objects"),
(3,1877,"Falls","Hail kills sheep in Texas","In Texas hail as large as a man's hand falls and kills thousands of sheep. Fort uses the Monthly Weather Review to list ice masses reported falling from the sky.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1877","large as a man's hand, killing thousands of sheep, Texas, May 3, 1877"),
(4,1821,"Cosmos","A star-like light on the Moon, again","The Rev. M. Ward and Francis Bailey again see a star-like light on the Moon, the same sort of light that Kater and Olbers had seen in February. Fort lists lunar lights that came and went.","NL","New Lands, citing Mems. R. A. S., 1-159","seen again, May 4th and 6th, by the Rev. M. Ward and by Francis Bailey"),
(6,1892,"Cosmos","A shining point on Venus","A correspondent to the English Mechanic sees a shining point, 'not polar', on Venus. Fort notes a similar unknown luminous object near Venus that August.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 55-310","upon May 6, 1892, he saw a shining point (not polar) upon Venus"),
(7,1867,"Cosmos","The beacon of Aristarchus","Tempel of Marseilles sees the beacon-like light of Aristarchus while the crater lay on the dark part of the Moon. Fort says it was a long time since the previous appearance.","NL","New Lands, citing Astronomical Register, 5-220","the beacon-like light of Aristarchus-observed by Tempel, of Marseilles"),
(8,1802,"Falls","A block of ice in Hungary","A mass of ice three feet long, three feet wide and more than two feet thick falls in a storm in Hungary. Fort puts it with other ice falls as possible data of a 'Super-Sargasso Sea'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Thomson, Introduction to Meteorology","that fell, in a storm, in Hungary, May 8, 1802"),
(10,1902,"Sky","Little suns over South Devon","Col. Markwick tells the English Mechanic that a friend saw in the sky over South Devon a great number of highly coloured objects like little suns or toy balloons. 'Altogether beats me,' says Markwick.","NL","New Lands, citing English Mechanic, 75-417","upon May 10, 1902, a friend of his had seen in the sky, in South Devon"),
(11,1894,"Falls","A turtle falls at Bovina","In a hailstorm, a piece of alabaster falls at Vicksburg, Mississippi, and a gopher turtle falls at Bovina, eight miles away. The Monthly Weather Review reported it.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1894","fell a gopher turtle"),
(12,1811,"Falls","Ice lumps in Derbyshire","Lumps of ice a foot in circumference fall in Derbyshire. Fort notes a cuboidal ice mass fell at Birmingham 26 days later, and uses the cases as clues to a 'Super-Sargasso Sea' above the Earth.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Annual Register, 1811-54","Lumps of ice, a foot in circumference, Derbyshire, England, May 12, 1811"),
(13,1870,"Cosmos","Twenty-seven lights on Plato","Birt reports an 'extraordinary display' on the Moon's crater Plato, where Pratt counts 27 lights. Birt thought the lights were not an effect of sunlight.","NL","New Lands, citing Birt","27 lights were seen by Pratt"),
(14,1864,"Falls","The Montauban stones","Twenty masses, some as large as a human head, of a substance that resembled dull-coloured earthy lignite fall at Montauban, France. This is the Orgueil fall, whose carbonaceous matter was contested.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Dr. Walter Flight","Montauban, France, May 14, 1864 (twenty masses"),
(15,1879,"Sea","Luminous waves in the Persian Gulf","Commander J. E. Pringle of H.M.S. Vulture notices luminous waves or pulsations in the water moving at great speed. Fort sets it with other 'wheels' and waves of light at sea.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing the Hydrographer of the British Navy","May 15, 1879, he had noticed luminous waves or pulsations in the water"),
(16,1833,"Falls","Dead fishes at Futtepoor","A fall of fishes at Futtepoor, India: 'They were all dead and dry.' Dr. Buist recorded that some weighed one and a half pounds and others three. Fort uses it to argue against the 'fish from nearby ponds' explanation.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Dr. Buist, Living Age, 52-186","A fall of fishes at Futtepoor, India, May 16, 1833"),
(17,1830,"Falls","A stone from the Comrie sky","A stone falls at Perth, 22 miles from Comrie, inside the 'earthquake region' where sounds and shocks were long reported. Fort cites Fletcher's List.","NL","New Lands, citing Fletcher's List, p. 100","A stone fell, May 17, 1830"),
(18,1883,"Falls","Strange stones in a tornado at Hillsboro","'A number of stones of peculiar formation and shapes, unknown in this neighborhood' fall in a tornado at Hillsboro, Illinois. Fort puts it with pebbles that might be from aerial beaches.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1883","fell in a tornado at Hillsboro, Ill., May 18, 1883"),
(19,1919,"Mysteries","Wireless messages while Hawker was at sea","While Harry Hawker is lost at sea, wireless operators pick up untraceable messages, meaningless in any language of Earth. Dispatches to the newspapers interpreted them as signals from elsewhere.","NL","New Lands, citing newspaper dispatches","Upon May 19, 1919, while Harry Hawker was at sea, untraceable messages"),
(20,1903,"Cosmos","A body on the terminator of Mars","Prof. Lowell describes a body seen on the terminator of Mars in the Lowell Observatory's first bulletin. A week later it was only 'suspected', and had moved 300 miles: 'probably a dust cloud'.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Lowell Observatory Bulletin No. 1","a body that was seen on the terminator of Mars, May 20, 1903"),
(22,1884,"Falls","Flinty stones at Bismarck","At 9 p.m. sharp sounds are heard throughout Bismarck, Dakota, caused by flinty stones striking windows. Fifteen hours later another fall of flinty stones occurs.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1884-134","at 9 o'clock, in the evening of May 22, 1884, sharp sounds were heard throughout the city"),
(23,1878,"Mysteries","A barn and horse vanish in a tornado","In a Wisconsin tornado 'a barn and a horse were carried completely away, and neither horse nor barn, nor any portion of either have since been found.' Fort cites the Monthly Weather Review.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1878","In a tornado, in Wisconsin, May 23, 1878"),
(25,1869,"Falls","Black stones in Wolverhampton","After a severe storm a great number of small black stones are found in the streets of Wolverhampton, the same as those that fell at Birmingham the year before. Symons's Meteorological Magazine reported it.","NL","New Lands, citing Symons' Met. Mag., 4-137","a great number of small, black stones had been found in the streets of Wolverhampton, May 25, 1869"),
(27,1884,"Falls","Tysnas turf torn by a falling meteorite","At Tysnas, Norway, a meteorite is said to have fallen and torn up the turf. Two days later 'a very peculiar stone' was found nearby. Fort cites Nature.","BD","Book of the Damned, citing Nature, 30-300","May 27, 1884, at Tysnas, Norway, a meteorite had fallen"),
(28,1883,"Sky","Changing scenes in the sky over Finsbo","At Finsbo, Sweden, the sky shows scenes of mountains, lakes and farms that change at short intervals. Fort lists them with the Swedish sky-pictures of 1882-85, including a town with a castle.","NL","New Lands, citing Monthly Weather Review, May 1883","at Finsbo, Sweden-changing scenes, at short intervals"),
(29,1868,"Falls","Stones fall on Birmingham for an hour","Thomas Plant writes in the Birmingham Daily Post that stones fell on Birmingham from the sky for an hour on the morning of May 29. Fort notes that stones kept falling for days before and after.","NL","New Lands, citing Birmingham Daily Post, May 30, 1868","morning of May 29, 1868, stones fell, in Birmingham, from the sky"),
(30,1867,"Sky","Mr. Bird's unknown forms","About 11 a.m., Mr. Bird sees unknown forms in the sky and through a telescope focused on them and Venus they appear twice the size of Venus. He wrote to the Birmingham Daily Post.","NL","New Lands, citing Birmingham Daily Post, May 31, 1867","about 11 A. M., May 30, he saw unknown forms in the sky"),
(31,1838,"Falls","Warm water falls on Geneva","Lukewarm water in large drops falls from the sky at Geneva. Fort lists it with other falls of warm water from clear skies, including Geneva in 1842.","NL","New Lands, citing Year Book of Facts, 1839-262","upon May 31, 1838, lukewarm water in large drops, fell from the sky, at Geneva"),
]
